[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. φ is smooth, σ∘φ = id_R, so S is a smooth R-algebra with a section.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. I = Ker(σ), so we have S/I ≅ R (via σ inducing an iso S/I → R, with inverse [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mφ).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. The conormal module I/I² is a module over S/I ≅ R, so it's an R-module. Given[0m[38;2;124;124;124mit's free of rank d.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. Since φ is smooth and has a section, S is locally a polynomial algebra. More [0m[38;2;124;124;124mprecisely, smooth + section means S is "formally smooth" and the completion [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlooks like a power series ring.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe standard approach: This is essentially the structure theorem for smooth [0m
[38;2;124;124;124malgebras with a section. The key result is that when S is smooth over R with a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msection σ, the I-adic completion of S is isomorphic to R[[t₁,...,t_d]] where d =[0m[38;2;124;124;124mrank(I/I²).[0m

